  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Exploring Implementation Approaches**\n\nI\'ve got it! Now I\'m figuring out how to build the frontend and backend. I\'m focusing on how MediaPipe actually works with standard web-based frameworks. I\'ve realized that using NPM packages is probably the best approach or loading the CDN script libraries. So far, running MediaPipe Pose and Face Mesh directly in React is proving reliable.\n\n\n**Prioritizing CDN Integration**\n\nI\'m now prioritizing CDN integration for this project. Loading `@mediapipe/pose` and `@mediapipe/face_mesh` directly from jsDelivr, either in `index.html` or dynamically in components, looks like the most robust path. These options avoid NPM bundler issues, especially with Vite. I can access them on the `window` object (e.g., `window.Pose`, `window.FaceMesh`). This simplifies the setup compared to NPM packages that have known CommonJS/ESM compilation problems in Vite.\n\n\n**Verifying Essential Dependencies**\n\nI\'m now cross-referencing the required CDN URLs. I\'ve pinpointed URLs for `camera_utils`, `control_utils`, `drawing_utils`, and `pose.js` and confirmed the `face_mesh.js` URL. I\'m investigating potential dependencies on a `tasks-vision` library but I\'ve decided to keep the classic libraries due to their relative simplicity, ease of development, and faster performance in simple situations. I also have confirmed landmark points in Pose.\n\n\n**Calculating Posture Angles**\n\nI\'m now integrating posture calculations. I\'ve pinpointed landmark indices for hips (23, 24), knees (25, 26), and ear/shoulder points from `results.poseLandmarks`. I\'m using the `calculateAngle` function to determine Ear-Shoulder-Hip angles. I\'m focusing on the left and right sides to flag posture issues, ensuring accurate assessments. The code is running fine!\n\n\n**Refining Level Checks**\n\nI\'m now refining the level checks. For \'Uneven Stance,\' I am now calculating the absolute vertical difference between left and right shoulders. I am testing it with normalized coordinates, assuming a 5% difference in the coordinate space ($0.0$ to $1.0$) is $0.05$. Therefore I am now defining `Math.abs(leftShoulder.y - rightShoulder.y) > 0.05`.\n\n\n**Calculating Smile Rate**\n\nI\'m now calculating a \"Smile Rate\" from Face Mesh. I\'m measuring the distance between mouth corners, using landmarks 61 and 291 (or, possibly, another set). I will need to refine the expression calculations further, but for the moment, I am focused on the distance, and the relative distance with other facial landmarks.\n\n\n**Defining Smile Metric**\n\nI am now focusing on refining the smile score. I\'ve chosen landmarks 61 and 291 for mouth corners and landmarks 33 and 263 for eye corners. I can now compute `mouthWidth`, using distance calculations between landmarks. I can calculate a `faceScale` metric. Finally, I will compute the `smileScore` by dividing the former by the latter. Now I should be able to determine the smile rate!\n\n\n**Calibrating Smile Score**\n\nI\'m now implementing the smile score calibration. I\'ve defined a formula to translate the `smileScore` into a percentage: `smileRate = Math.min(100, Math.max(0, ((smileScore - 0.42) / 0.18) * 100))`. I am excited to see this in practice!\n\n\n\n**Assessing Gaze Direction**\n\nI\'m now writing an algorithm for gaze direction based on relative iris position within the eyes. I\'m focusing on calculating horizontal offset and normalizing it by eye width for both eyes to account for camera distance. I\'ve pinpointed landmarks for eye corners and iris centers. I am excited to see the code work!\n\n\n**Evaluating Gaze and Approach**\n\nI\'m now calculating gaze direction with high accuracy. I\'m leveraging eye corner and iris center landmarks for horizontal and vertical offsets, normalizing by eye width. My plan is to use those offsets to compute an eye contact score directly. I will also develop an approachability metric by measuring the distance between eyebrows to see how relaxed the face is.\n\n\n**Defining Approachability Metric**\n\nI am now focusing on refining the approachability score based on eyebrow position, and integrating that into my existing system. I am computing the brow distance, using inner eyebrow landmarks such as 107 and 336 (or, potentially 70 and 300), and normalizing it with the distance between eye corners (landmarks 33 and 263). A browDistance relative to face scale gives the approachabilityScore: a furrowed brow decreases the normalized distance, lowering approachability. I am finalizing the formula to translate the score into a 0-100% feedback system!\n\n\n**Designing API and Frontend**\n\nI\'m now creating the `/api/analyze-grooming` endpoint in my backend using Express and the `@google/genai` SDK. I\'m focusing on parsing the base64 image from the request body to send it to Gemini. I\'ll need to send the clean webcam frame, not the one with the skeleton. I\'m planning to display Gemini\'s grooming feedback neatly on the front-end.\n\n\n**Implementing JSON Response**\n\nI\'m now fully focused on a JSON response. I\'m using `ai.models.generateContent` with `responseMimeType: \"application/json\"` and `responseSchema` for clean JSON responses from Gemini! This will ensure structured output from my API, which will be essential for the front-end. I will create `server.ts` to implement the server side including Vite middleware and the `/api/analyze-grooming` route.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 24
      nanos: 238015227
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
