  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Designing React Components**\n\nOkay, I\'m thinking about the React component structure now. I\'ve decided to prioritize modularity and elegant design. Currently, I\'m focusing on these components: `GroomingSection.tsx`, which will display the Gemini grooming analyzer results with a countdown, and another for `DataDisplay.tsx`, which will show the raw data, and maybe `UserPreferenceSection.tsx`.\n\n\n**Defining Component Layouts**\n\nI\'ve been solidifying component layouts! I\'m now certain about including `PostureMetricsPanel.tsx` with live dials and level visualizations, the `FacialEngagementPanel.tsx` that will display key facial metrics, and the `SessionHistory.tsx`, which will visualize progress with Recharts. I\'ve also solidified `WebcamMirror.tsx` and the other components mentioned. I\'m focusing on their functions now!\n\n\n**Structuring the MediaPipe Loop**\n\nOkay, I\'m now structuring the MediaPipe Pose and FaceMesh loops. I\'ve concluded they can be combined or run sequentially from a single `requestAnimationFrame` loop. I will trigger them using the MediaPipe `Camera` utility or a custom `requestAnimationFrame` loop on the HTML5 `<video>` element, which gives better customizability, resilience, and pause/resume functionality. I will test `@mediapipe/camera_utils`.\n\n\n**Exploring MediaPipe Integration**\n\nI\'m now diving into the MediaPipe integration, examining the `camera_utils` for video input. It looks good; I can see how to initialize a `Camera` instance and manage the `onFrame` callback. I\'m considering using canvas overlays for landmark visualization, leveraging `drawConnectors` and `drawLandmarks`. I\'m starting to think about how to structure the drawing process.\n\n\n**Synchronizing Landmark Rendering**\n\nI\'ve hit a conceptual breakthrough: to avoid drawing conflicts between Pose and FaceMesh, I\'ll combine the landmark data or store the latest landmarks, rendering both in a synchronized draw loop. This solves the problem of callbacks clearing each other\'s work on the canvas. Further, rendering the video to the canvas itself is the key to creating a high-fidelity \"Virtual Mirror\" overlay! This enables uniform scaling and advanced canvas filters, such as contrast and color grading.\n\n\n**Implementing Unified Rendering**\n\nI\'ve got it! Utilizing `useRef` hooks for `latestPoseRef` and `latestFaceRef` I\'ll create a single, unified render loop to synchronize video frames and both sets of landmarks seamlessly. I will draw the mirrored video frame and both the pose and face landmarks with custom colors to avoid flickering. This is the optimal approach!\n\n\n**Refining Coordinate Transformations**\n\nI\'m now refining the coordinate transformations, realizing that if the video is mirrored, landmark drawing still works, because the coordinates are based on the original input. This is critical for the virtual mirror! I will use this knowledge for the pose drawing. I will draw skeletal connections in gold, with navy blue rings for key joints.\n\n\n**Refining Face Landmark Rendering**\n\nI\'m now implementing the refined face landmark rendering, drawing the face mesh, irises, and lips with custom colors to be highly visible and appealing. I am using `window.drawConnectors`, applying transparency to the mesh for a subtle effect, and solid gold for the iris/eyes, and gold with transparency for the lips. This looks wonderful!\n\n\n**Calculating Pose Angles**\n\nI\'ve got clean virtual mirror mirroring and custom brand styling working perfectly! Now I am focused on the landmark index points needed for Pose and Face landmark-based metric computations. Specifically, I\'m thinking about the Ear-Shoulder-Hip angle calculation, focusing on averaging the visibility scores of both sides of the body to select the optimal landmarks for the angle calculation.\n\n\n**Evaluating Posture Metrics**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on posture metrics! I have verified the Ear-Shoulder-Hip angle for slouch detection; a 180-degree angle indicates perfect vertical alignment. Less than 165 indicates slouching; exactly as expected! I\'m also now calculating the shoulder level difference as a percentage of image height; values greater than 5% perfectly identify uneven stance. This is a big win.\n\n\n**Defining Facial Metrics**\n\nI\'m now identifying critical face landmark indexes for calculating eye, mouth, and eyebrow metrics. I\'m noting the right and left eye corners (inner and outer) for calculating gaze. Importantly, I\'m developing a robust parser for Iris landmark detection, with a safe fallback to Pupil Center or average eye corner coordinates if Iris data is unavailable, to ensure the application remains functional. The parsing function is clean.\n\n\n**Refining Metric Calculations**\n\nI\'m now refining facial metric calculations! The iris fallback works great; it ensures the application remains robust. I\'ve defined landmark indexes for eye corners and mouth. I\'m developing a \'smile metric\' by normalizing mouth width by eye distance for a robust, user-agnostic calculation. This \'smileMetric\' is calibrated to standard resting mouth width.\n\n\n**Calculating Smile Metrics**\n\nI\'m now calculating smile metrics! I\'ve calibrated a `smileMetric` using mouth width and eye distance, with a standard resting mouth width baseline (0.40). Smile widening yields a value above 0.50, and I\'ve mapped this to a smooth `smileRate`. I am now working on a smooth and responsive function that accurately maps this.\n\nI am also finding the pupil/iris position relative to eye horizontal limits to calculate eye contact! We will normalize the pupils with `lhsPct` and `rhsPct` to get a standardized value for gaze deviations.\n\n\n**Scoring Engagement Metrics**\n\nI\'m now implementing the gaze and approachability scoring system, which feels great! I\'m scoring eye contact, using normalized pupil position deviations with thresholds for a direct gaze. I am also calculating the brow distance to assess approachability, and I\'ve derived `eyeContactScore` and `approachabilityScore` that work!\n\n\n**Modularizing Core Components**\n\nI\'m now modularizing the core components based on the established structure! I\'m planning to create helper components for a Header, PostureMetricsPanel, FacialEngagementPanel, GroomingSection, and SessionHistory. This aligns perfectly with the requirement to avoid consolidating logic. I\'m prioritizing the Header\'s layout and functionality, aiming for a premium feel with airline branding and status indicators, using `lucide-react` icons.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 30
      nanos: 669905300
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
