  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining Session Initiation**\n\nI\'m focusing on \"Start Session\" functionality. It\'ll connect the MediaPipe pipeline. That means camera activation, overlay triggers, and, if selected, the automatic snapshot timer will begin. That\'s the core of how the user\'s training session starts, and I\'m homing in on the interaction.\n\n\n**Finalizing Session Termination**\n\nI\'ve been working on the \"End Session\" functionality and how to implement it cleanly. I\'ve decided to use a `sessionActive` state variable. Setting it to `false` creates an intuitive welcome state. When active, the camera and MediaPipe run, and a visual cue appears: a red/green \"END\" button. Clicking this stops the session, sets `sessionActive` to false, shuts down the camera and MediaPipe, and displays a summary state.\n\n\n**Refining Camera Management**\n\nI\'m now integrating smooth camera restarts with the \"START SESSION\" button. After examining `App.tsx`\'s pipeline setup (lines 190-285), I\'ve identified the critical `useEffect`. By including `sessionActive` in the dependency array, I can dynamically allocate and deallocate resources. When `sessionActive` is `false`, MediaPipe and the camera won\'t run. Toggling to `true` initializes everything, while switching back to `false` triggers cleanup. I am working on the exact logic for the cleanup function.\n\n\n**Confirming State and Cleanup**\n\nI\'ve streamlined the camera cleanup within the `useEffect`. It stops the camera and closes the MediaPipe pipelines when `sessionActive` becomes `false`. I\'ve added a state variable, `sessionActive`, to explicitly control the session\'s lifecycle. Now, the logic flow is clean, with the camera active when `sessionActive` is `true` and off otherwise. I\'ve confirmed that the cleanup functions as expected. I\'m focusing on preserving the final session metrics as a final report card.\n\n\n"
